    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d780">

                <pb facs="0809" n="495" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg495"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 195.2/252</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        French Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Jusserand</hi>)</head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-02">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">October 2, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of the 3d ultimo<anchor n="1" xml:id="d780-f1"/> in which
                    by order of your Government you repeat its views on the question of the proposed
                    purchase by the United States Government of German vessels which, by reason of
                        <pb facs="0810" n="496" xml:id="pg496"/> the pending conflict in Europe, are
                    now immobilized in ports of the United States. You express the opinion of your
                    Government that such a transaction is without precedent and could not in any
                    respect be regarded as legitimate on the part of a great neutral country. You
                    state at length the reasons upon which this opinion of your Government is
                    based.</p>
                <p>In response to your note I have the honor to state that this Government is giving
                    the matter presented by your excellency its attentive consideration, and further
                    that, while it refrains from commenting at the present moment upon the opinions
                    of your Government, as expressed in your communication, this Government reserves
                    for the present, the statement of its views as to the contentions of the
                    Government of the French Republic.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[See also the British Embassy’s memorandum of October 1, 1914,<anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d780-f2"/> concerning the transfer of the German ship <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Alexandria</hi> (renamed <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Sacramento</hi>), for the statement: “the British Embassy wishes to
                        state that there is no desire or intention to object to the transfer to the
                        American flag.”]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
